  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
    "lesson_core_move": "Trace how an input contribution $x(\\tau)$d $\\tau$ at time $\\tau$ produces the time-invariant system response $x(\\tau)$$h(t-\\tau)$d $\\tau$, justifying $h(t-\\tau)$ as the impulse response shifted to each impulse location in the convolution integral.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convolution is built by tracking one tiny input contribution at a time. The key question in this lesson is: if an input contribution is located at time $\\tau$, what shifted copy of the impulse response does it produce?",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the basic impulse-response fact for an LTI system. If the input is the unit impulse at time $0$, the output is the impulse response $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t) \\longmapsto h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now move the impulse to a fixed time $\\tau_0$. Because the system is time-invariant, shifting the input impulse by $\\tau_0$ shifts the output response by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-\\tau_0) \\longmapsto h(t-\\tau_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the convolution integral, the impulse location is not one fixed number. The variable $\\tau$ scans through all possible input times, so the response to an impulse located at $\\tau$ is $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The input does not contribute a plain unit impulse at time $\\tau$. It contributes a small weighted impulse with weight $x(\\tau)d\\tau$, so the shifted response is scaled by that same weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)d\\tau\\,\\delta(t-\\tau) \\longmapsto x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $h(t-\\tau)$ appears because it is the impulse response shifted to match the input contribution located at time $\\tau$. The factor $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)d\\tau$ is one output contribution, and convolution accumulates all such contributions over every $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Tracing the Response to an Impulse at $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$, identify the output produced by the input $\\delta(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-2)$. This is the unit impulse moved from time $0$ to time $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this LTI system, the response to the unit impulse at time $0$ is the impulse response $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t) \\longmapsto h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the system is time-invariant, shifting the input impulse by $2$ shifts the output response by the same amount. The shift stays in the same form: $t-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-2) \\longmapsto h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output produced by the input $\\delta(t-2)$ is $h(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Scaling the Shifted Response by $x(\\tau)d\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$, write the output contribution from the weighted impulse $x(2)\\Delta\\tau\\,\\delta(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-2)$. From the previous section, an unweighted impulse at time $2$ produces the shifted response $h(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-2) \\longmapsto h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now the impulse is multiplied by the scalar weight $x(2)\\Delta\\tau$. Because the system is linear, the same scalar multiplies the output response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(2)\\Delta\\tau\\,\\delta(t-2) \\longmapsto x(2)\\Delta\\tau\\,h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output contribution from $x(2)\\Delta\\tau\\,\\delta(t-2)$ is $x(2)\\Delta\\tau\\,h(t-2)$. The shift is still determined by the impulse location; the new factor only scales the shifted response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Reading $h(t-\\tau)$ at a Fixed Output Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "With the output time fixed at $t=5$, rewrite the shifted impulse-response factor $h(t-\\tau)$ as a function of $\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase \"output time fixed at $t=5$\". This means $t$ is held at one output time, while $\\tau$ remains the variable input location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the shifted response factor from the convolution integrand.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $5$ only for the fixed output time $t$. Do not replace $\\tau$, because $\\tau$ still scans the possible input locations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t-\\tau)\\big|_{t=5}=h(5-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, with the output time fixed at $t=5$, the response factor becomes $h(5-\\tau)$. This is the shifted response value observed at output time $5$ from each input location $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Why $h(t-\\tau)$ Appears",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Connecting Weighted Shifted Responses to the Convolution Integrand",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the convolution integrand that comes from accumulating the weighted shifted-response contributions $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)d\\tau$ over all input times $\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the word integrand. We are not computing the output yet; we are identifying the expression that will be accumulated over $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from one output contribution caused by the small input piece at time $\\tau$. The input weight is $x(\\tau)d\\tau$, and the shifted response factor is $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this contribution, $d\\tau$ is the small accumulation width. The integrand is the factor multiplying that width.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau=\\bigl[x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\bigr]\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the convolution integrand is $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$. It combines the input value at the scanning time with the impulse response shifted to match that same input location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{integrand}=x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Accumulating all of these infinitesimal contributions over every $\\tau$ gives the LTI output. The integral sign belongs to the full accumulation, not to the integrand by itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
